## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to handle mixed text and JSON logs, manage log rotation and compression, and perform cross-host aggregation with time-based filtering. It tests regex parsing, time-windowing, deduplication logic, and working within POSIX shell constraints without external dependencies.

## Key Concepts
- POSIX shell constraints with gzipped logs and mixed formats
- Time-window filtering for the last 30 minutes
- Robust user extraction from both plaintext and JSON logs
- Case-insensitive deduplication with per-host aggregation

## Code Example
```bash
for host in $(awk -F'[/=]' '/^\/(logs|var)\/log\// && !/^#/ {print $3}' /etc/hosts 2>/dev/null | sort -u); do
  users=$(find "/logs" -name '*.log*' -mmin -30 2>/dev/null | \
    xargs -I{} sh -c 'zcat -f "{}" 2>/dev/null' | \
    awk -v host="$host" '
      BEGIN {now=systime(); cutoff=now-1800}
      /^[[:space:]]/ {next}
      /^"?[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2} [0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}/ {
        gsub(/[\[\]:]/, "", $1" "$2)
        cmd="date -d \""$1" "$2"\" +%s 2>/dev/null || date -d 0 +%s"
        cmd | getline ts; close(cmd)
        if (ts >= cutoff) {
          if ($0 ~ /ERROR|WARN/) {
            for (i=1; i<=NF; i++) {
              if ($i ~ /^user[=:]/) {
                gsub(/[,:"\047]/, "", $(i+1))
                print tolower($(i+1))
              }
            }
          }
        }
        next
      }
      /^"?\{/ {
        if (match($0, /"ts"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"([^"]+)"/, t)) {
          cmd="date -d \""t[1]"\" +%s 2>/dev/null || date -d 0 +%s"
          cmd | getline ts; close(cmd)
          if (ts >= cutoff && match($0, /"user"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"([^"]+)"/, u)) {
            print tolower(u[1])
          }
        }
        next
      }
    ' | sort -u | wc -l)
  echo "$host: $users unique users"
done
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to handle mixed log formats with rotation, per-host aggregation, and time-windowed analysis using standard UNIX tools.

## Key Concepts
- Mixed log formats (standard access logs and JSON lines)
- Handling both .log and .log.gz files
- Graceful handling of unreadable files
- Time parsing and windowing in shell (epoch conversion)
- AWK associative arrays for per-host tallies
- Robust parsing of multi-line error blocks without double-counting

## Code Example
```bash
find /logs/nginx -type f -name '*.log*' -print0 | while IFS= read -r -d '' f; do
  if [[ "$f" == *.gz ]]; then
    zcat "$f" 2>/dev/null || continue
  else
    cat "$f" 2>/dev/null || continue
  fi
done | awk -v cutoff="$(date -d '2 hours ago' +%s)" '
/^ERROR:/ { error_block=1; next }
error_block && /^[[:space:]]/ { next }
error_block { error_block=0 }
{
  if (match($0, /\{"ts": *([0-9]+)/, ts)) {
    timestamp = ts[1]
  } else if (match($0, /^([0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3})/, host)) {
    split($0, parts, /\[/)
    if (match(parts[2], /([0-9]{2}\/[A-Za-z]{3}\/[0-9]{4}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2})/, date_match)) {
      cmd = "date -d '\''" date_match[1] "\'' +%s"
      cmd | getline timestamp
      close(cmd)
    }
  }

  if (timestamp && timestamp >= cutoff) {
    if (match($0, /"status": *([45][0-9]{2})/, status)) {
      counts[host[1]]++
    } else if (match($0, /\s([45][0-9]{2})\s/, status)) {
      counts[host[1]]++
    }
  }
}
END {
  for (h in counts) print h ":" counts[h]
}' | sort
```

